## Byte Streams: FileInputStream and FileOutputStream

FileInputStream reads bytes and FileOutputStream writes bytes. The read method returns -1 at end of stream. For larger files, use a byte buffer.

## Read loop and buffer length

`InputStream.read(byte[])` returns the number of bytes actually read, or -1 at end of stream. Always write only that returned count; the last buffer may be partly filled. A zero-length read request is a special case, so use a non-empty buffer for the normal copy loop.

```java
byte[] buffer = new byte[8192];
int count;
while ((count = input.read(buffer)) != -1) {
    output.write(buffer, 0, count);
}
```

## Resource and failure handling

Open both streams with try-with-resources. Test an empty file, a file shorter than the buffer and one spanning multiple buffers. For a reliable copy, compare file sizes and bytes rather than printing binary content as text.
